(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-cms-exam-3-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 3 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A student asks how the site of a salivary tumour relates to the chance that it is malignant. What is the relationship?","choices":["The smaller the gland, the higher the proportion that are malignant","Only the parotid gives rise to malignant tumours","The larger the gland, the higher the proportion that are malignant","Site has no bearing on the likelihood of malignancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Parotid tumours are 75 to 80 per cent benign, submandibular 50 to 60 per cent, and minor salivary glands only about 35 per cent benign. The relationship runs inversely with gland size, which is a memorable rule and changes how aggressively a small-gland lesion is investigated. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 139"},{"q":"An audiogram of a 45-year-old factory worker shows a notch at 4000 hertz with recovery at 8000 hertz. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ototoxicity","Meniere disease","Noise-induced hearing loss","Presbycusis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cumulative noise exposure classically produces a notch centred around 4000 hertz with some recovery at higher frequencies, which is what distinguishes it from presbycusis. Presbycusis declines progressively as frequency rises and does not recover, so the shape of the curve rather than its depth makes the diagnosis. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man needs wax removed. He has a history of a tympanic membrane perforation repaired years ago. What is the most appropriate method of removal?","choices":["Cotton bud removal by the patient at home","Softening drops alone for two weeks","Warm water irrigation","Microsuction or curette under direct vision"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Irrigation is contraindicated when the drum may not be intact, because water forced into the middle ear seeds it with canal organisms and causes a suppurative otitis media. Removing the wax under direct vision keeps the procedure entirely within the canal and lets the operator see the drum as it is uncovered. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 28"},{"q":"A clinician is describing a cervical node and considers which features suggest malignancy. Which features are they?","choices":["Tenderness and rapid enlargement","Immobility and non-tenderness","Bilateral distribution","Softness and fluctuance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The descriptors that matter are: size, shape, mobility, consistency and tenderness. An immobile node suggests fixation to surrounding structures by tumour, and non-tenderness points away from an inflammatory cause, since inflamed nodes hurt. Persistence or continued enlargement is what triggers fine needle aspiration. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 93"},{"q":"A clinician is taught that age changes the prior probability when assessing a neck mass. How does age change it?","choices":["Age is not a useful discriminator in neck masses","Above 40 most masses are congenital","Under 40 most masses are malignant","Under 40 most are inflammatory, except Hodgkin lymphoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most neck masses in patients under 40 are inflammatory in nature, and names Hodgkin lymphoma as the specific exception to that rule. Above 40 the probability shifts toward malignancy, which is why age over 40 appears on the red-flag list alongside size, duration, firmness and tobacco use. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has facial pain, black necrotic tissue visible on the nasal turbinate, and rapidly progressive periorbital swelling. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A fungus ball in the maxillary sinus","Acute bacterial sinusitis with orbital extension","Allergic fungal sinusitis","Invasive fungal sinusitis, needing debridement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Black necrotic mucosa in an immunocompromised or diabetic patient indicates angioinvasive fungal disease, where hyphae invade blood vessels and cause tissue infarction. The blood supply is destroyed, so antifungal drugs alone cannot reach the dead tissue; surgical debridement is required alongside systemic therapy and control of the diabetes. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 10-year-old has a two-day sore throat with a temperature of 101 degrees Fahrenheit, tonsillar exudate and tender anterior cervical nodes. She has no cough. A rapid antigen detection test is negative. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Send a throat culture before deciding about antibiotics","Accept the negative test and treat symptomatically","Order antistreptolysin O titres to make the diagnosis today","Start penicillin immediately without further testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her Centor score is high, with a point each for absence of cough, exudate, fever and tender anterior nodes, plus one for her age. Rapid antigen tests are specific but not sufficiently sensitive, so a negative result in a child with a high clinical probability is always confirmed by culture rather than accepted. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman describes a whooshing sound in her right ear that keeps time with her pulse. It has been present for two months and is louder when she lies down. Her hearing is subjectively normal. How should this symptom be characterised?","choices":["A symptom of Meniere disease","An expected consequence of presbycusis","Pulsatile unilateral tinnitus, which requires investigation for a vascular cause","Ordinary subjective tinnitus requiring only reassurance and sound therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most tinnitus is a non-pulsatile bilateral ringing that accompanies sensorineural loss and needs no imaging. Tinnitus that is unilateral or synchronous with the pulse is the red flag combination, because it suggests turbulent blood flow or a vascular middle ear lesion such as a glomus tumour, and those need to be found. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 5-year-old has chronic mouth breathing, snoring and hyponasal speech with bilateral nasal obstruction. There is no discharge. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Adenoidal hypertrophy","Nasal foreign body","Deviated septum","Nasal polyposis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The adenoid sits in the nasopharynx behind the nasal cavity, so enlargement obstructs both sides equally and produces mouth breathing, snoring and the hyponasal voice of a blocked nose. The bilateral, discharge-free pattern is what distinguishes it from a foreign body or infection. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has had gradually worsening hearing over three years, worse since her pregnancy. She says she hears conversation better in a noisy restaurant than in a quiet room. Otoscopy is normal. Her father had a similar problem. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acoustic neuroma","Otosclerosis","Presbycusis","Otitis media with effusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hearing better in background noise, called paracusis, happens because a conductive loss filters out low-frequency ambient noise more than speech, and because people raise their voices in noisy rooms. Combined with a normal drum, a family history, worsening in pregnancy, and gradual conductive loss, this is the classic picture of stapes fixation. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 65"},{"q":"A clinician is asked how acute, subacute and chronic rhinosinusitis are separated. How are they separated?","choices":["Acute under twelve weeks, chronic beyond six months","Duration is not used to classify rhinosinusitis","Acute under one week, chronic beyond four weeks","Acute under four weeks, chronic beyond twelve weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute disease resolves within four weeks, subacute occupies the interval between four and twelve, and chronic is defined by symptoms persisting beyond twelve weeks. The boundaries matter because the mechanism shifts from a single infection to impaired mucociliary clearance with persistent inflammation, and so does the treatment. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 12"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has left ear pain. His father says he has been swimming almost daily in a neighbour's pool this summer. Examination shows erythema and oedema of the left ear canal and pain on manipulation of the pinna. The tympanic membrane is not well visualised but appears intact, and the right ear is normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral amoxicillin suspension","Topical ciprofloxacin-dexamethasone","Topical clotrimazole 1%","Topical triamcinolone 0.1%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pain on moving the pinna or tragus localises the disease to the CANAL, not the middle ear, and water exposure macerates the canal skin and raises its pH so that Pseudomonas and Staphylococcus overgrow. Treatment therefore has to reach the canal surface: a topical fluoroquinolone covers those organisms, and the added steroid shrinks the oedema so the drops can actually get past it. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 40"},{"q":"A newborn fails the hearing screen. Examination shows a white forelock, widely spaced medial canthi and heterochromia of the irises. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nonsyndromic hereditary hearing loss","Waardenburg syndrome","Alport syndrome","Usher syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Waardenburg is a neural crest disorder, and the neural crest contributes both to the melanocytes of the skin, hair and iris and to the stria vascularis of the cochlea. That shared origin is why pigmentary abnormalities and sensorineural deafness appear together, and it is the syndrome the described features define. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 62"},{"q":"A clinician is teaching the single most useful bedside manoeuvre for distinguishing otitis externa from acute otitis media. Which manoeuvre is it?","choices":["Check for fever","Move the tragus or pinna","Perform a Weber test","Look for discharge in the canal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pain on manipulating the pinna or tragus localises the disease to the canal, because moving those structures moves the inflamed canal skin. Middle ear infection sits behind an intact drum and is unaffected by moving the external ear, so the manoeuvre separates the two before the otoscope is even used. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman is struck on the side of the head with an open hand. She has immediate ear pain, a brief episode of bleeding from the canal, and reduced hearing. Otoscopy shows a small central perforation with clean edges. There is no vertigo and the facial nerve is intact. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Irrigate the canal to remove blood clot","Refer for immediate tympanoplasty","Keep the ear dry and review","Start oral antibiotics for all traumatic perforations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A small traumatic perforation with clean edges and no vertigo or facial nerve deficit implies the ossicular chain and inner ear are intact. The drum has good spontaneous healing capacity, so the treatment is to protect it: keep water out so bacteria do not reach the middle ear through the hole, and allow it to close on its own. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with untreated HIV has a rapidly enlarging cervical mass with purple discolouration of the overlying skin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Kaposi sarcoma","Idiopathic follicular hyperplasia","Reactive viral lymphadenopathy","Persistent generalised lymphadenopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Kaposi sarcoma is one of the conditions that must be excluded in an HIV-positive patient with lymphadenopathy, alongside tuberculosis, Pneumocystis and lymphoma. Its vascular nature produces the characteristic purple appearance, which distinguishes it from the follicular hyperplasia that causes most adenopathy in this group. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has a bead in the left nostril. The child is calm and the object is visible anteriorly. What is the most appropriate first attempt at removal?","choices":["A parent-delivered positive pressure blow","Grasp the bead with forceps","Irrigate the nostril with saline under pressure","Refer immediately for removal under general anaesthesia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The parent seals the child's mouth with their own and blows while the unaffected nostril is occluded, so the pressure travels through the posterior choana and pushes the object forward. It is atraumatic, needs no instruments, and avoids the risk of pushing the bead deeper that instrumentation carries in a small child. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has severe sore throat, odynophagia and a muffled voice for a day. He is febrile but not drooling and has no stridor. Fiberoptic laryngoscopy shows a swollen epiglottis. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Discharge on oral antibiotics with review in 24 hours","Lateral neck radiography before any treatment","Admit for observation, intravenous antibiotics and airway monitoring","Immediate tracheostomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adult supraglottitis progresses more slowly than the paediatric form and often does not require immediate intubation, so observation with humidification, glucocorticoids, intravenous antibiotics and nebulised adrenaline is appropriate. The airway is monitored because intubation or tracheostomy becomes necessary if it obstructs. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man of southern Chinese origin has a firm neck mass, intermittent double vision and numbness of the left cheek, with unilateral nasal obstruction and occasional blood-stained nasal discharge. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","Chronic bacterial sinusitis","Allergic rhinitis","Nasal polyposis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The combination of a neck mass, cranial nerve involvement giving diplopia and facial numbness, and unilateral nasal symptoms is the described presentation. The tumour sits in the nasopharynx where it drains early to cervical nodes and can invade the skull base, and it is strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus and endemic in southern China. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl has had a foul-smelling discharge from the left ear for a week. She has been afebrile with no ear pain. Otoscopy after suctioning reveals a small piece of foam rubber impacted against the drum. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Otomycosis","Retained foreign body with infection","Malignant otitis externa","Chronic suppurative otitis media"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An organic or porous object retained in the canal obstructs drainage and acts as a nidus, so the canal skin becomes secondarily infected and produces the foul discharge. The absence of pain and fever, with a unilateral offensive discharge in a young child, is the pattern that should prompt a careful search for something in the canal. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 47"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman has a rapidly enlarging hard thyroid mass with hoarseness and difficulty swallowing that has developed over six weeks. Biopsy shows undifferentiated giant and spindle cells. What is the diagnosis, and what is the prognosis?","choices":["Medullary carcinoma","Primary thyroid lymphoma arising in Hashimoto thyroiditis","Papillary carcinoma, with an excellent prognosis","Anaplastic carcinoma, with a prognosis measured in months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anaplastic carcinoma is about 1 per cent of thyroid cancers, occurs in elderly patients, and is composed of small cell, giant cell and spindle cell types. It is the most aggressive form, typically causing death in 6 to 36 months, and is resistant to all treatment modalities, which is why the rapid growth and compressive symptoms are characteristic. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man attends a loud concert and afterwards notices muffled hearing and ringing in both ears. By two days later his hearing has returned to normal, though the ringing lingers briefly. What does this represent?","choices":["Acoustic trauma from a single impulse noise","Early presbycusis unmasked by the noise","A temporary threshold shift, which recovers within 24 to 48 hours","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss requiring urgent referral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intense sound temporarily fatigues the cochlear hair cells, raising the threshold at which sound is detected, and function returns as the cells recover over 24 to 48 hours. The clinical importance is that repeated temporary shifts eventually become permanent, so the recovery is a warning rather than a reassurance. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 44"},{"q":"A student asks why acute otitis media can progress to mastoiditis but not, for example, to sinusitis. What is the correct explanation?","choices":["The eustachian tube drains into the mastoid","The mastoid shares a wall with the paranasal sinuses","Infection spreads haematogenously to the mastoid","The mastoid air cells communicate directly with the middle ear"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The mastoid antrum opens into the epitympanum through the aditus, so the mastoid air cells and the middle ear form one continuous mucosa-lined space. Infection therefore extends into the mastoid by direct continuity, which is why mastoiditis is the classic suppurative complication of untreated otitis media. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man has a painless, fluctuant swelling in the submandibular region that has been slowly enlarging. Biopsy shows filamentous organisms. What is the treatment of choice?","choices":["Antituberculous therapy","Penicillin","Amphotericin B","Surgical excision alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Actinomycosis presents as a painless fluctuant mass in the submandibular or upper digastric region, and the combination of painlessness with fluctuance is what distinguishes it from an ordinary pyogenic abscess, which would be exquisitely tender. Diagnosis is clinical and by biopsy, and penicillin is the treatment. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with chronic rhinosinusitis is told the underlying problem is impaired mucociliary clearance. He asks what that means. What does this mean?","choices":["The mucus has become too thin to trap organisms","The cilia can no longer move the mucus blanket out of the sinus","The sinus ostium has been surgically closed","The immune system no longer produces antibodies to sinus organisms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sinus mucosa is lined with ciliated epithelium that sweeps a mucus layer toward the ostium in a fixed direction. Chronic inflammation damages the cilia and thickens the mucus, so secretions stagnate in the sinus, which perpetuates inflammation and invites repeated infection. That is why chronic disease behaves differently from a single acute infection. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with otosclerosis asks about the risk to her children. What should she be told?","choices":["It is caused by recurrent middle ear infection in childhood","It is purely acquired from noise exposure","It often runs in families and may worsen during pregnancy","It has no hereditary component at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Otosclerosis has a strong familial tendency, which is why a family history supports the diagnosis. Progression during pregnancy is also recognised, which is relevant counselling for a woman of childbearing age who is deciding when to have surgery. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man who smokes heavily and uses his voice loudly at work has six months of hoarseness. Laryngoscopy shows a single pedunculated lesion on the left vocal fold with visible vascular markings. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Observation with repeat laryngoscopy in a year","Speech therapy alone","A course of oral corticosteroids","Microlaryngoscopic excision"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A polyp is unilateral and pedunculated, which distinguishes it from bilateral symmetric nodules, and it is commoner in men who smoke and abuse the voice. Excision both treats it and provides tissue, which matters because a large polyp may hide an occult early laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma in exactly this risk group. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient with reduced hearing has a tympanogram showing a shallow peak at normal middle ear pressure. What does this tympanogram indicate?","choices":["A stiff mechanism, as in ossicular fixation","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Fluid in the middle ear","Ossicular discontinuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peak compliance occurring at normal pressure means the middle ear is ventilating properly, so the eustachian tube is working. A shallow peak means the system does not move much at any pressure, which indicates stiffness rather than fluid. That is the type As pattern and points to ossicular fixation such as otosclerosis, or to tympanosclerosis. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 19-year-old student has recurrent painful oral ulcers with yellow-grey centres and red halos, each under a centimetre, appearing on the inner cheek and inner lip and healing in about a week. They recur during examination periods. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpetic gingivostomatitis","Minor aphthous ulceration","Herpetiform aphthous ulceration","Major aphthous ulceration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Minor aphthae are under a centimetre, are the commonest form, burn and tingle before they appear, and heal in 7 to 10 days without scarring. Their location is diagnostic: they occur on freely moving, NON-keratinised mucosa such as buccal and labial mucosa, and stress is a recognised precipitant. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 16"},{"q":"A clinician palpates a neck mass and finds it fluctuant. What does this suggest?","choices":["A metastatic lymph node","A lipoma","A cystic lesion","A vascular lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fluctuance means the mass transmits pressure as a fluid would, so it contains liquid rather than solid tissue. That is one of the four rules of thumb, alongside pulsatility indicating vascular, years of stability indicating benign, and rapid growth indicating infection or lymphoma. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has a squamous cell carcinoma of the tonsil that is human papillomavirus positive. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Surgical resection with radiotherapy, which gives better functional outcomes","Chemotherapy alone as first-line treatment","Observation, since human papillomavirus related tumours regress","Surgical resection alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oropharyngeal tumours are treated with resection plus radiotherapy, and radiotherapy gives better functional outcomes in this site, where speech and swallowing are at stake. Between 60 and 80 per cent of oropharyngeal cancers are human papillomavirus related, which is also relevant to prognosis. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 7-year-old has a unilateral, solitary, tender anterior cervical node following a sore throat. Which organism is most likely responsible?","choices":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Bartonella henselae","Staphylococcus aureus, which accounts for the majority","Beta-haemolytic streptococcus, which accounts for about 70 per cent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Local infections of the ear, nose and throat drain to regional nodes, and about 70 per cent of these unilateral solitary anterior cervical nodes relate to beta-haemolytic streptococcal infection. Around 20 per cent are staphylococcal including MRSA, and 10 per cent are viral, atypical mycobacterial or Bartonella. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 52-year-old professional trumpet player has hoarseness, cough and a sensation of a foreign body in the throat. Laryngoscopy shows a smooth dilation at the level of the false cord. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vocal cord papillomatosis","Laryngocele","Laryngeal carcinoma","Vocal cord polyp"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A laryngocele is abnormal dilation or herniation of the laryngeal saccule, and sustained raised intraluminal pressure from wind instrument playing is a classic association. The smooth dilation at the level of the false cord on laryngoscopy is the characteristic finding, and computed tomography confirms the diagnosis and defines its extent. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman is brought by her husband, who says she never listens to him. She admits she sometimes asks people on her right to repeat themselves. She denies dizziness, headache and visual change. Her only medication is furosemide. Weber lateralises to the left. On the left, air conduction lasts 15 seconds and bone conduction 10 seconds; on the right, air conduction lasts 22 seconds and bone conduction 10 seconds. What is the most likely cause?","choices":["Meniere disease","Cerumen impaction","Ototoxicity from the diuretic","Otosclerosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both ears have air conduction longer than bone conduction, which is the normal Rinne pattern and is also what a sensorineural ear gives, so the loss must be sensorineural rather than conductive. Weber lateralising to the left means the RIGHT is the worse ear, matching her symptom. Loop diuretics are ototoxic, and that is the only exposure in the history. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man is struck on the ear with an open hand and has immediate pain, hearing loss and vertigo, with blood in the canal. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Irrigate the canal to clear the blood","Assess urgently for inner ear injury as well as a perforation","Reassure and review in two weeks, as most perforations heal","Prescribe vestibular suppressants and discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vertigo after ear trauma is the finding that changes the picture. A simple perforation does not cause vertigo, so its presence raises the possibility of ossicular disruption with a perilymphatic fistula or direct labyrinthine injury, which needs prompt specialist assessment rather than routine review. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has a breathy hoarse voice and occasional coughing while drinking, six weeks after thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy shows one immobile vocal fold. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Unilateral paralysis from recurrent laryngeal injury","Vocal cord nodules from postoperative voice strain","Bilateral vocal cord paralysis","Laryngeal oedema from intubation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs immediately behind the thyroid and is at risk during thyroidectomy. A unilateral paralysis leaves one cord unable to meet the other, which gives the breathy voice from air escaping and allows aspiration during swallowing, hence the coughing on drinking. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a mass in the upper neck that swells at mealtimes. What does this suggest?","choices":["A metastatic lymph node","A salivary rather than a nodal cause","A branchial cleft cyst","A thyroid nodule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Swelling that varies with eating implicates a salivary gland, because eating stimulates salivary flow and an obstructed gland distends. Lymph nodes and neoplasms do not change size over minutes, so the timing of the swelling localises the problem before any imaging is arranged. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman on immunosuppression after transplantation has persistent cervical lymphadenopathy. Bacterial cultures are negative and antibiotics have failed. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start empirical antituberculous therapy","Increase the antibiotic spectrum","Send fungal cultures and serology","Reduce the immunosuppression and observe"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immunocompromised patients are particularly susceptible to fungal infection, most commonly Candida, Histoplasma and Aspergillus, and fungal cultures AND serology are required to make the diagnosis. Negative bacterial cultures with antibiotic failure in this host is precisely the setting in which to look. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 35"},{"q":"A patient asks which frequencies are affected earliest in Meniere disease. Which frequencies are they?","choices":["Low frequencies","High frequencies","Only frequencies above 8000 hertz","All frequencies equally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The distension of endolymphatic hydrops is greatest at the cochlear apex, which is the region that codes low frequencies, so the early audiogram shows a low-frequency sensorineural loss that fluctuates. That pattern distinguishes it from almost every other sensorineural cause, which start high. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man is diagnosed with a thyroid carcinoma arising from parafollicular C cells. Beyond his own treatment, the clinician arranges an additional action involving his relatives. What additional action should be taken?","choices":["Screen family members for papillary carcinoma","Screen family members for Hashimoto thyroiditis","No family screening, since thyroid cancer is not heritable","Screen family members for multiple endocrine neoplasia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Medullary carcinoma arises from the calcitonin-producing C cells and carries a familial association with multiple endocrine neoplasia. Because relatives may carry the same predisposition and can be identified before disease develops, screening the family is part of the management of the index case rather than an optional extra. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man wakes to find he cannot hear from his right ear. There is no pain, discharge or trauma. Otoscopy is normal. Weber lateralises to the left and Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone conduction bilaterally. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent referral for sudden sensorineural loss","A course of oral antibiotics for presumed otitis media","Microsuction of the canal for presumed wax","Reassurance and review in six weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal otoscopy with Weber lateralising AWAY from the affected ear and a normal Rinne pattern in both ears establishes the loss is sensorineural rather than conductive. Sudden sensorineural loss is a syndrome rather than a diagnosis, and it is time-critical: the chance of recovery falls the longer treatment is delayed, so referral is prompt rather than routine. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man with a week of sinusitis symptoms now has swelling and erythema around the right eye, pain on eye movement and double vision. He is febrile. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent imaging and admission for intravenous antibiotics","Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate and review in 48 hours","Topical decongestants and nasal steroid","Ophthalmology referral for the diplopia alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Periorbital swelling with painful restricted eye movement and diplopia means the infection has crossed from the sinus into the orbit, because the ethmoid sinus is separated from it only by the paper-thin lamina papyracea. That is orbital cellulitis, which threatens vision and can spread intracranially, so it needs imaging and intravenous therapy now. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has chronic unilateral nasal obstruction, snoring, and recurrent nosebleeds from the more open side. Examination shows a markedly deviated septum. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Nasal packing to reduce the nosebleeds","Antihistamines","Septoplasty","Long-term intranasal corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A deviated septum narrows one passage and diverts airflow through the other, and that accelerated dry airflow cracks the mucosa on the open side, which is why the nosebleeds come from there rather than from the obstructed side. Only straightening the septum corrects the airflow, so surgery is the definitive treatment when symptoms justify it. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with long-standing Hashimoto thyroiditis develops a rapidly enlarging thyroid mass. Fine needle aspiration is reported as showing a lymphoid infiltrate that cannot be confidently distinguished from her known thyroiditis. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Total thyroidectomy","Accept the result as Hashimoto and continue thyroid hormone replacement","Repeat the fine needle aspiration with more passes","Proceed to open biopsy, because aspiration cannot separate lymphoma from Hashimoto"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Primary thyroid lymphoma is most often a non-Hodgkin B cell tumour arising on a background of Hashimoto thyroiditis, and both conditions fill the gland with lymphocytes. Aspiration cytology cannot reliably tell a reactive infiltrate from a monoclonal one, so tissue architecture is needed, followed by lymphoma staging. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 32-year-old competitive surfer has bilateral, symmetrical, smooth bony swellings narrowing both ear canals. He reports recurrent trapped water and occasional infections. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Osteoma","Cerumen impaction","Exostoses","Cholesteatoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Repeated exposure to cold water stimulates new bone formation in the canal walls, which is why the condition is known as surfer's ear and why it is bilateral and symmetrical. The clinical consequence is a narrowed canal that traps water and debris, producing the recurrent otitis externa he describes. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman with rapidly progressive bilateral sensorineural loss is suspected of having autoimmune inner ear disease. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Hearing aids alone","Immediate cochlear implantation","Observation with repeat audiometry in a year","A trial of systemic corticosteroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Autoimmune inner ear disease is one of the few sensorineural losses that responds to treatment, and the response to systemic corticosteroids serves as both therapy and a diagnostic test, since the condition is otherwise hard to confirm. That responsiveness is the reason it must be considered before a bilateral progressive loss is written off. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman on warfarin has a nosebleed that has stopped with pressure. Her international normalised ratio is 6.2. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Correct the anticoagulation as well as treating the nose","Admit for immediate posterior packing","Treat the nose locally and continue warfarin unchanged","Stop warfarin permanently"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clotting studies are not routine in epistaxis but are indicated in anticoagulated patients, and this result is well above range. Treating only the local bleeding point leaves the systemic reason it bled and the risk of rebleeding or of haemorrhage elsewhere entirely unaddressed. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has fever, sore throat, neck stiffness and refusal to eat for two days. He is now drooling and leaning forward with his neck extended. A lateral neck radiograph shows widening of the prevertebral soft tissue. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Peritonsillar abscess","Bacterial pharyngitis","Epiglottitis","Retropharyngeal abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Suppuration of retropharyngeal nodes after an upper respiratory infection is a disease of children under five, and the widened prevertebral space on a lateral film is the classic radiographic sign. It is a surgical emergency because the space runs from skull base to posterior mediastinum, and mediastinitis carries a 50 per cent mortality. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has nasal crusting, whistling on inspiration and recurrent minor nosebleeds. Examination shows a septal perforation. He has used intranasal steroid for years for allergic rhinitis. What is the most likely cause?","choices":["Nasal polyps eroding the septum","Untreated allergic rhinitis itself","Chronic ischaemia from a topical agent","A previous undiagnosed nasal fracture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The anterior septum has a thin mucosa over cartilage with no other blood supply, so anything that causes sustained vasoconstriction or mucosal injury there can perforate it. Prolonged intranasal steroid, particularly if sprayed at the septum rather than laterally, and cocaine are the two named causes, and the history has to ask about both. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had a discharging left ear for four months. It has not responded to three different topical antibiotics. The discharge is now blood-stained and he has developed deep aching pain. Otoscopy shows friable tissue in the canal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Systemic antifungal therapy","Biopsy of the canal lesion","A fourth course of topical antibiotics with a different agent","Aural toilet and observation for six weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Otitis externa that will not respond to appropriate treatment, together with bloody otorrhoea and friable tissue, is the described presentation of canal carcinoma. Only histology distinguishes malignancy from chronic inflammation or granulation, and the treatment pathway is entirely different, so tissue has to be obtained rather than another drop tried. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 71"}]};
